    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "ACE inhibitors and ARBs (angiotensin receptor blockers) are both effective first-line medications for high blood pressure (hypertension), providing similar reductions in blood pressure and protection against major cardiovascular events, though ARBs are generally better tolerated.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "ARBs are generally better tolerated"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Efficacy and Comparison"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Blood Pressure Control: Both classes lower blood pressure equally well by inhibiting the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), which relaxes and widens blood vessels.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Hard Outcomes: Large clinical analyses show little to no difference between ACE inhibitors and ARBs regarding overall mortality, heart attacks, strokes, or kidney disease progression.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Guidelines: Major organizations\u2014such as the American College of Cardiology (ACC), American Heart Association (AHA), and European Society of Cardiology (ESC)\u2014historically listed ACE inhibitors and ARBs as co-equal first-line options (alongside calcium channel blockers and thiazide diuretics). However, recent real-world data and guidelines increasingly favor ARBs due to a more favorable safety profile .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Differences and Side Effects"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Cough: A persistent, dry cough is a well-known side effect of ACE inhibitors (caused by the buildup of bradykinin). This side effect does not occur with ARBs.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Angioedema: Both classes carry a rare risk of angioedema (severe tissue swelling, particularly of the face or throat), though the risk is significantly lower with ARBs.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Adherence/Withdrawal: Because ARBs cause fewer annoying side effects like cough, patient discontinuation and treatment withdrawal rates are lower compared to ACE inhibitors.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Combination Therapy: You should never take an ACE inhibitor and an ARB together. Dual blockade does not improve outcomes and dramatically increases the risk of side effects like low blood pressure, high potassium (hyperkalemia), and kidney damage .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Read the full analysis in JAMA Network .",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "JAMA Network",
            "link": "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2784663"
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          2
        ]
      },
      {
